! C22-6578 PROBLEM 3.9, "DOUBLE-PRECISION COMPARE", printed pp.32-34
! (PDF 33-35), read in the ink.
!
!   "The accumulator contains a double precision floating-point
!   quantity.  Another double precision floating-point quantity is
!   stored in two full words, with the more significant part in M1, less
!   significant part in M1+1.  Compare the two double precision
!   quantities and set the appropriate indicators $AE, $AL and $AH."
!
!   Method 1.  Full-scale double-precision subtract followed by a
!   test on the result.
!       MKOMP   ST(U), A1               'save accumulator
!               SLO (U), A1+1.
!               DL(U), A1+1.            'double-precision subtract
!               D-(N), M1+1.
!               D+(N), A1
!               D-(N), M1
!               L(BU, 3), $RLZ          '$RLZ, $RZ, $RGZ fetched
!               ST(BU, 3), $AL          '$AL, $AE, $AH stored
!               DL(U), A1+1.            'restore accumulator
!               D+(U), A1
!               BEW, $
!       A1      DR(U), (2)
! The printed statements follow the deck's own two, which load the
! accumulator, and come before the deck's data, as printed: spacing,
! trailing periods and all.  The page defines neither M1 nor the
! accumulator's value; the deck's are below.
!
! ASSEMBLED WITH ASM=strap2 (see problem_3_1.strap; "SLO (U)" with its
! space assembles as SLO(U)).
!
! WHAT THE PROGRAM DOES, with the page of the rule:
!   ST(U), A1 and SLO(U), A1+1. save the accumulator as two words:
!     STORE, Reference Manual printed p.90; STORE LOW ORDER, p.97, "The
!     exponent is obtained by subtracting 48 from the exponent in bits
!     0-11 of the accumulator", the fraction is accumulator bits 60-107.
!   The double-precision subtraction is Figure 28's (p.97), the order of
!     its notes: low parts first, then add A1, then subtract M1.  ADD
!     DOUBLE, p.95; LOAD DOUBLE, p.96.  The last D-(N) sets RLZ, RZ or
!     RGZ "as in ADD" on the 96-bit difference.
!   L(BU, 3), $RLZ fetches indicator bits 56-58 (RLZ, RZ, RGZ) into the
!     accumulator's right end, offset 0: accumulator bits 125-127.
!   ST(BU, 3), $AL stores them into bits 60-62 (AL, AE, AH).
!   DL(U), A1+1. / D+(U), A1 restore the accumulator.  LOAD DOUBLE:
!     "Accumulator bits 60-107 are set to zero.  Accumulator bits 108-127
!     remain unchanged" (p.96); ADD DOUBLE: "Accumulator bits 108-127
!     remain unchanged" (p.95).  So the THREE BITS THE PROGRAM FETCHED
!     STAY IN ACCUMULATOR BITS 125-127, word 9's last three bits, and
!     the deck checks them there: 4 = 100 = less, 2 = 010 = equal,
!     1 = 001 = greater.
!
! >>> THE COMPARISON INDICATORS THEMSELVES ARE NOT CHECKED: THE MODEL
! DROPS THE STORE INTO THEM. <<<  ST(BU, 3), $AL changes nothing on
! the model (bsv/StretchStorage.bsv, writeWord: stores to word 11 are a
! documented SIMPLIFICATION, dropped silently).  Reproducer:
! decks/problem_3_vfl_store_to_indicators.strap; docs/NEXT.md
! "C22-6578 CHAPTER 3" item 6.  tests/run_tests.sh carries the
! indicator word's expected value as a comment, to be restored when the
! store is implemented.
!
! THIS DECK'S DATA AND RESULT, HAND-DERIVED (tests/problem_3_expected.py
! p39_run follows the same rules and agrees):
!   THE PAGE'S OWN EXAMPLE, comment (a), with E = 1:
!     accumulator   (E, .111...1)  and  (E-48, .1000...0)
!                   = (1 - 2**-49) x 2**E
!     M1, M1+1      (E+1, .1000...0)  and  (E+1-48, .000...0)
!                   = 2**E
!   The page: a high-order compare alone says AE; the program must say
!   AL.  By Figure 28's order: (E-48, .1) - (E-47, 0) = (E-48, .1)
!   normalized; + (E, .111...1): .111...1 with a one in bit 49;
!   - (E+1, .1): the accumulator shifts right one place, .0111...1|1,
!   less .1 is -2**-49 of 2**(E+1): negative, RLZ.  AL.
!     A1         002FFFFFFFFFFFF0   (exponent 1, fraction all ones)
!     A1+1.      05F8000000000000   (exponent -47, .1)
!     word 8     002FFFFFFFFFFFF8   (restored; bits 60-63 are the low
!                                    fraction's leading 1000)
!     word 9     0000000000000004   (bits 64-107 zero; 100 = RLZ, less)
!     indicator word, held back: 0000000000000028 (AL, bit 60, and RGZ,
!       bit 58, from the restoring D+(U) of a positive number)
!     PC 78, the BEW.
!
! INJECTION, OBSERVED 2026-11-16 (data side, on a copy of the deck, the
! deck itself untouched): M1 changed from 1X2 to 281474976710655X1, so M
! is A's high part with a zero low part and A is GREATER.  FAIL
! verdict_acc_right only (got 1, AH); A1, A1+1, word 8 and the PC passed.

! --- the deck's own: load the accumulator with (AHI, ALO) ---
START   DL(U), AHI
        D+(U), ALO
! --- the printed program ---
MKOMP   ST(U), A1
        SLO (U), A1+1.
        DL(U), A1+1.
        D-(N), M1+1.
        D+(N), A1
        D-(N), M1
        L(BU, 3), $RLZ
        ST(BU, 3), $AL
        DL(U), A1+1.
        D+(U), A1
        BEW, $
A1      DR(U), (2)
! --- the deck's data ---
M1      DD(N), 1X2
        DD(N), 0.0X-46
AHI     DD(N), 281474976710655X1
ALO     DD(N), 1X-47
        END, START
